{"id": 96, "ts": 1780161032.912203, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the strongest overall fit.\u202fWe now have concrete evidence that C \u201ccreates a positive atmosphere with his crew,\u201d which directly addresses the crew\u2011cooperation criterion and shows he can contribute constructively in the cockpit.\u202fAt the same time, C \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis,\u201d \u201cmakes quick correct decisions,\u201d and \u201cunderstands complicated technology,\u201d covering stress resilience, decision quality, and technical competence\u2014core requirements for long\u2011distance operations.\nThe only notable drawback is his \u201cdifficulty communicating ideas,\u201d which could hinder information accuracy and professional communication.\u202fHowever, the positive crew atmosphere suggests he can still work effectively with others despite this limitation, and his calm, decisive style may compensate in high\u2011stress situations.\u202fHis reluctance to pursue further education hints at limited openness to feedback, but this has not yet manifested as a safety risk in the observations we have.\nCompared with the alternatives:\n- Candidate\u202fA shows strong leadership and situational anticipation but is seen as a \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d and \u201cnot open to new ideas,\u201d raising concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability.\n- Candidate\u202fB handles stress well but makes \u201cnasty remarks about his colleagues,\u201d indicating poor professional communication and likely crew\u2011cooperation issues.\n- Candidate\u202fD takes responsibility and solves problems but is described as \u201cmoody,\u201d a \u201cloner,\u201d and has a \u201chot temper,\u201d all of which threaten calmness under pressure and crew interaction.\nGiven C\u2019s balanced strengths across the most safety\u2011critical criteria and the only remaining concern being communication difficulty\u20